<div class="kick">HubSpot · Make · Gemini · MailerLite · Twilio</div>
<h1>AI Lead Scoring &amp;<br>Lifecycle Automation</h1>
<p class="lede">Three case studies: an AI lead scoring system that routes every HubSpot lead to sales or marketing,
a B2B nurture programme that converted 8% of a cold list into paying enrolments, and a
behaviour-branched cart recovery system built with email and SMS.</p>
<div class="by">Diana Rettob · GTM Engineer · AI Lead Scoring &amp; Automation on HubSpot</div>

<!-- ============ CASE 0 ============ -->
<h2><span class="num">01</span> — AI Lead Scoring &amp; Routing</h2>
<span class="tag demo">Practice project · sample data · Make + HubSpot + Gemini</span>

<h3>The problem it solves</h3>
<p>Sales teams lose time deciding which new leads deserve a call. This system scores every new HubSpot
contact the moment it arrives and routes it to Sales or Marketing, so reps start with the best leads first.</p>

<h3>The flow</h3>
<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Make.com scenario: HubSpot, Gemini, parse, score, update, router">
<ul>
<li>Watch new HubSpot contacts</li>
<li>Gemini classifies seniority, function and company fit</li>
<li>Wait step to respect the free API rate limit</li>
<li>Parse the JSON answer</li>
<li>Score with fixed points</li>
<li>Write score and status back to HubSpot</li>
<li>Route: 80 and above to Sales, below 80 to Marketing</li>
</ul>

<h3>The scoring rubric</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Factor</th><th>Points</th></tr>
<tr><td>Seniority</td><td>C-level 40 · Director 32 · Manager 22 · Individual 10 · Intern 0</td></tr>
<tr><td>Function</td><td>Sales 30 · Marketing 20 · Tech 10 · Other 0</td></tr>
<tr><td>Company fit</td><td>B2B SaaS, ideal size 30 · other SaaS 20 · B2B non-SaaS 10 · not a fit 0</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Total 0 to 100. A lead scoring 80 or above becomes Sales Qualified.</p>

<div class="note">The decision that mattered: AI classifies, fixed rules calculate. Gemini only labels
the lead; Make does the arithmetic. The same lead always receives the same score, so sales can trust
and audit every number.</div>

<h3>Result</h3>
<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="15 sample leads scored and written back to HubSpot">
<p>15 sample leads were scored and written back to HubSpot automatically. All 15 scores matched my
hand-calculated values. Qualified and nurture leads are routed into static HubSpot segments that the
sales and marketing teams can open. Failed API calls are stored for retry, not discarded.</p>
<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="HubSpot segments: Sales Qualified and Marketing Nurture">

<h3>Next build</h3>
<p>Version 1 does not notify sales yet. Planned next:</p>
<ul>
<li>Email and HubSpot task to the lead owner</li>
<li>Alert when the workflow stops or fails</li>
<li>Routing by rep and product interest</li>
<li>Score calibration against closed-won deals</li>
</ul>

<!-- ============ CASE 1 ============ -->
<h2><span class="num">02</span> — B2B Lead Nurture Programme</h2>
<span class="tag real">Client work · Interscale · Built in HubSpot</span>

<h3>The problem</h3>
<p>Around <b>500 leads</b> had come in from Revit-related campaigns. They were sitting in the CRM
untouched: no segmentation, no follow-up sequence, and no way for sales to tell who was worth calling.
Everyone received the same message, or nothing at all.</p>

<h3>What I built</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Segmentation by sector (HubSpot lists)</b> — leads split by industry so each group received language relevant to their work, not generic product copy</li>
<li><b>Drip nurture sequence</b> — staged emails moving contacts from unaware to considering, rather than pitching on first contact</li>
<li><b>Engagement-based scoring</b> — opens and clicks used to identify genuine interest; only engaged leads were promoted to the deal pipeline</li>
<li><b>Automated task creation (HubSpot workflows)</b> — when a lead crossed the engagement threshold, HubSpot created a task for the right rep, so no hot lead sat idle</li>
<li><b>Reporting dashboards</b> — campaign performance, pipeline health, and lead status visible in one place</li>
</ul>

<h3>Result</h3>
<div class="metrics">
  <div class="m"><div class="big">8%</div><div class="lbl">Cold leads converted to paid enrolments</div></div>
  <div class="m"><div class="big">~40</div><div class="lbl">Enrolments from ~500 leads</div></div>
  <div class="m"><div class="big">&gt;10%</div><div class="lbl">Hot-lead close rate within one month</div></div>
</div>

<div class="note">The decision that mattered: leads were not pushed to sales on volume, but on
behaviour. Sales stopped calling cold names and started calling people who had already engaged —
which is where the close rate came from.</div>

<!-- ============ CASE 2 ============ -->
<h2><span class="num">03</span> — Cart Recovery: Email + SMS</h2>
<span class="tag demo">Build demonstration · MailerLite + Make + Twilio</span>

<h3>The problem it solves</h3>
<p>Most shoppers who add to cart never return. A single reminder email treats every one of them the
same — yet someone who <b>opened and ignored</b> has a different problem from someone who
<b>never saw the email at all</b>. The first has an objection. The second has an attention problem.
They need different responses, and different channels.</p>

<h3>The flow</h3>
<pre>Trigger — joins group "Cart Abandoned"
   |
   +-- Delay 1 hour        (not instant: feels like surveillance)
   |
Email 01 — Cart reminder   (no discount yet)
   |
   +-- Delay 24 hours      (gives the open time to happen)
   |
Condition — was Email 01 opened?
   |
   +-- YES -> Email 02B — objections answered, then free shipping
   |            |
   |            +-- Delay 48 hours
   |            |
   |          Email 03 — last call
   |            |
   |          Exit
   |
   +-- NO  -> SMS via Twilio (Make.com)  <-- channel switch
                |
              Exit

Exit condition: tag "purchased" removes the contact from the flow</pre>

<h3>Decisions behind it</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Decision</th><th>Reasoning</th></tr>
<tr><td>No discount in Email 01</td><td>Discounting within the first hour teaches customers that abandoning a cart earns a price cut. It damages margin permanently.</td></tr>
<tr><td>24-hour delay before the condition</td><td>Conditions evaluate the moment they are reached. Without the delay, nobody has had time to open, and every contact falls to the "No" branch.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Objections before incentive (Yes branch)</td><td>An open without a click signals hesitation, not price sensitivity. Returns policy and shipping time are addressed first; the incentive closes.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SMS only on the No branch</td><td>Sending more email to someone who ignored the first is wasted. Changing channel is what recovers attention — and it never overlaps with the email branch.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exit on purchase</td><td>Without it, paying customers keep receiving "your cart is waiting" — the fastest route to an unsubscribe.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Applying this to B2B</h3>
<p>The same logic applies to stalled B2B journeys: a prospect who opened a demo or trial email but did not
book is an objection to answer; one who never opened it needs a different channel, such as LinkedIn or a
call task for the rep. The branching, scoring and task automation are identical, built on HubSpot instead
of an e-commerce trigger.</p>

<h3>Technical scope</h3>
<ul>
<li>Behaviour-branched automation built in MailerLite (trigger, delays, conditions, exit rules)</li>
<li>Custom fields for product and price personalisation across every message</li>
<li>SMS delivered through Twilio, orchestrated via Make.com — API integration, not a native connector</li>
<li>Open and click tracking enabled to feed the branching logic</li>
</ul>

<!-- ============ TESTING ============ -->
<h2><span class="num">04</span> — How I Test</h2>

<p>Subject lines are tested as a hypothesis, not a coin flip. Each variant is written to answer a
question about the audience, so that the result teaches something regardless of which one wins.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Variant A</th><th>Variant B</th><th>What the result tells us</th></tr>
<tr>
  <td>You left something behind</td>
  <td>Still thinking it over?</td>
  <td>If B wins, the audience rejects transactional tone — apply that voice to the whole programme.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Free shipping — 24 hours</td>
  <td>A few things people ask before ordering</td>
  <td>If B wins, the barrier is doubt rather than price. That changes the entire retention strategy.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Last call — cart expires tonight</td>
  <td>Should we let this go?</td>
  <td>Time urgency versus loss aversion. The winner shows which motivator fits this audience.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<div class="note">The winning subject line matters less than the decision it produces. A test that does
not change what happens next was not worth running.</div>

<h3>Metrics tracked</h3>
<ul>
<li>Open rate and click-through rate per email and per branch</li>
<li>Branch distribution — what proportion reaches Yes versus No, which reveals whether the delay is set correctly</li>
<li>Conversion and revenue attributed to the flow</li>
<li>Unsubscribe rate per message, to catch sequences that push too hard</li>
</ul>

